---
/**
 * DEBUG-ONLY explorer. NOT SHIPPED.
 *
 * This is a dynamic route whose getStaticPaths returns ZERO paths in a
 * production build, so `npm run build` emits no HTML for it at all — the pages
 * exist only under `astro dev`. That is the whole reason for the [view] param:
 * a static `tokens.astro` would always be built. Every debug view routes
 * through here to inherit that guarantee — adding a `foo.astro` beside this
 * file would ship it. Delete this directory (and src/data/token-graph.ts,
 * src/data/component-promises.ts) when the refinement work it supports is done.
 *
 *   /debug/tokens      the three-tier graph + health checks
 *   /debug/components  the tier-3 surface as a promise, checked against delivery
 */
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Section from '@esa/docs/Section.astro';
import TokenGroupTable from './_TokenGroup.astro';
import PropertyLevel from './_PropertyLevel.astro';
import ComponentsView from './_ComponentsView.astro';
import type { TokenGroup } from '../../data/token-graph';

const VIEWS = ['tokens', 'components'] as const;

export function getStaticPaths() {
  // Production emits nothing.
  if (!import.meta.env.DEV) return [];
  return ['tokens', 'components'].map((view) => ({ params: { view } }));
}

const view = Astro.params.view as (typeof VIEWS)[number];

// Deliberately a DYNAMIC import inside the component body, not a static one at
// the top. A static import is evaluated whenever Astro loads this module to
// call getStaticPaths — including in a production build, where token-graph.ts
// would then hit the filesystem for a page that is never emitted. The body runs
// only when a route actually renders, and in production there are none.
const {
  tierCounts,
  primitiveGroups,
  componentGroups,
  undefinedRefs,
  duplicateDeclarations,
  adHocHooks,
  orphans,
  stagedSurfaces,
  chromeSurfaces,
  CHROME_EXEMPT,
  unusedRampSteps,
  tierSkips,
  semanticHardcodes,
  dimensionRoles,
  healthTotal,
  primitiveCategories,
  coreCategory,
  coreSetNotes,
  themePrimitiveOverrides,
  scanCoverage,
  scanCoverageGaps,
  READ_SCAN_EXEMPT,
} = await import('../../data/token-graph');

const t2 = await import('../../data/tier2-naming');
const t3 = await import('../../data/tier3-naming');
const t1 = await import('../../data/tier1-naming');

const TIER_NOTE = {
  component:
    'Tier 3 — the per-component theming surface, authored in packages/tokens/src/component-tokens.css. A spoke overrides these to diverge ONE component from the semantic default.',
  semantic:
    'Tier 2 — intent, compiled from tokens/semantic/*.json. This is where a spoke’s brand lives: re-point a role and every component reading it re-skins.',
  primitive:
    'Tier 1 — the raw ramps, compiled from tokens/primitive/*.json. These never move, not in the hub and not in a theme. "Used by" below is the blast radius if one ever did.',
} as const;

const total = tierCounts.primitive + tierCounts.semantic + tierCounts.component;
---

<DocsLayout title={view === 'components' ? 'Component promises (debug)' : 'Token graph (debug)'}>
  {view === 'components' && <ComponentsView />}
  {view === 'tokens' && (
  <article class="article tokens-debug">
    <h1>Token graph</h1>

    <p class="dev-banner">
      <strong>Debug page — not shipped.</strong> This route is emitted only under
      <code>astro dev</code>; <code>npm run build</code> produces no HTML for it. Values shown are the
      <code>:root</code> light defaults compiled into <code>@esa/tokens</code> — theme blocks are
      deliberately excluded so you see the shipped default chain, not one theme&rsquo;s overrides.
    </p>

    <p class="lede">
      Every token in the system, classified by the tier that <em>declares</em> it (source of truth:
      which directory the JSON lives in, not a name pattern), with its full resolution chain and its
      reverse dependencies — who points at it.
    </p>

    <div class="tally">
      <div class="tally-cell"><b>{total}</b><span>tokens</span></div>
      <div class="tally-cell t3"><b>{tierCounts.component}</b><span>tier 3 · component</span></div>
      <div class="tally-cell t2"><b>{tierCounts.semantic}</b><span>tier 2 · semantic</span></div>
      <div class="tally-cell t1"><b>{tierCounts.primitive}</b><span>tier 1 · primitive</span></div>
      <div class={`tally-cell ${healthTotal ? 'warn' : 'ok'}`}>
        <b>{healthTotal}</b><span>health findings</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="filter-bar">
      <input id="tok-filter" type="search" placeholder="Filter tokens… (e.g. surface, grass-9, dialog)" autocomplete="off" />
      <span id="tok-count" class="filter-count"></span>
    </div>

    <Section id="health" title="Health">
      <p>
        Tripwires the per-component &ldquo;Theming surface&rdquo; table can&rsquo;t see, because each is a
        statement about the graph rather than about one component. Three of these are
        <b>inventory, not defects</b> — dimension roles, ad-hoc hooks, and unused ramp steps — and are
        excluded from the headline number. Folding them in would make the tally permanently alarming
        and therefore ignorable.
      </p>

      <h3 class="sub">Scan coverage — where &ldquo;nothing reads this&rdquo; is measured from</h3>
      <p class="cat-note">
        Every check below that says a token is unread is only as good as the set of files this
        module opens. That set is now explicit and rendered here, because it has been wrong three
        times: <code>--duration-0</code> (its only reader was an <code>@media</code> block),
        <code>--topbar-*</code> (filed &ldquo;staged&rdquo; for months), and most recently
        <b>102 <code>--typography-*</code> tokens</b> reported as orphans while
        <code>packages/tokens/src/typography.css</code> read them 169 times. An unscanned surface
        that ships is now itself a health finding.
      </p>
      <table class="findings">
        <thead><tr><th>Root</th><th>Kind</th><th>Files</th><th>Tokens read</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {scanCoverage.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td><code>{r.rel}</code></td>
              <td><span class={`slot-flag f-${r.kind === 'component' ? 'match' : 'extra'}`}>{r.kind}</span></td>
              <td class="num">{r.files}</td>
              <td class="num">{r.tokens}</td>
              <td class="dim">{r.why}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
          {READ_SCAN_EXEMPT.map((e) => (
            <tr class="row-zero">
              <td><code>{e.rel}</code></td>
              <td><span class="slot-flag f-absent">not scanned</span></td>
              <td class="num">—</td>
              <td class="num">—</td>
              <td class="dim">{e.why}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>

      {[
        { id: 'scancov', label: 'Shipped surfaces nobody scans', rows: scanCoverageGaps, note: 'A .css file @esa/tokens exports that no scan root covers and no exemption names. Every token it reads will report as an ORPHAN, and orphan on this page reads as “delete it”. Derived from the package’s own exports map rather than a hand-kept list, so adding a partial without rostering it turns this red on the next build. Third occurrence of this class — see the SCAN_ROOTS comment in token-graph.ts.', severity: 'bad' },
        { id: 'undef', label: 'Undefined references', rows: undefinedRefs, note: 'Declared nowhere AND read with no fallback, so the declaration drops entirely. Unambiguous bugs.', severity: 'bad' },
        { id: 'themeprim', label: 'Theme re-points a primitive', rows: themePrimitiveOverrides, note: 'SPEC.md: “Primitives never move — not in the hub, not in a theme.” themes.css says the same in its own header. Moving a primitive moves everything downstream of it, which is the exact blast radius the tier model exists to prevent. Re-point the semantic or component token instead — which is what both shipped themes now do, re-skinning shape through --radius-surface rather than --radius-200.', severity: 'bad' },
        { id: 'dupes', label: 'Declared at two tiers', rows: duplicateDeclarations, note: 'The same name declared in the token JSON and again in component-tokens.css. The tier-3 copy loads last and wins, so the compiled declaration is dead while still reading as authoritative in the source JSON. It also breaks the tier model — the token is both “never moves” and “a per-component hook”. Pick one tier.', severity: 'bad' },
        { id: 'skips', label: 'Tier-3 color skipping tier 2', rows: tierSkips, note: 'A component color token wired straight to a primitive. A spoke re-pointing the semantic layer will NOT re-skin these.', severity: 'warn' },
        { id: 'hardcode', label: 'Semantic colour hardcodes', rows: semanticHardcodes, note: 'A tier-2 COLOUR terminating in a raw literal without passing through a ramp — a magic value hiding at the intent layer, off the palette and unconstrained by it.', severity: 'warn' },
        { id: 'dimroles', label: 'Dimension roles (tier 2, no ramp behind them)', rows: dimensionRoles, note: 'Tier-2 dimension tokens that hold a literal rather than aliasing a primitive — control heights, chip heights, layout widths. Not a defect: no tier-1 ramp exists for these, so tier 2 is where the definition belongs. Inventory, so that if one ever grows a ramp it can move onto it.', severity: 'info' },
        { id: 'orphans', label: 'Orphan hooks (tier 2 + 3)', rows: orphans, note: 'Declared, but nothing reads it — no token, no component, and no other shipped surface (see the Scan coverage table above for exactly which files that means). Either dead surface to fold away, or a hook nobody wired up. Primitives are excluded here — see “unused ramp steps” below. Staged surfaces are excluded too — see the next check.', severity: 'warn' },
        { id: 'staged', label: 'Staged surfaces (component not built yet)', rows: stagedSurfaces, note: 'Unread because the component that will read them does not exist yet — the contract is declared first so it can be reviewed before code depends on it. Governed by STAGED_PREFIXES in token-graph.ts; each prefix must be documented in SPEC.md and have a component doc page. Removing a prefix from that list should mean the component landed, not that the finding got annoying.', severity: 'info' },
        { id: 'chrome', label: 'Chrome, exempt by decision (component EXISTS)', rows: chromeSurfaces, note: `Not staged and not dead: the component is built and these map onto real elements in it, and we are choosing not to wire them. Kept in its own bucket because --topbar-* spent months filed as "staged" — which reads as "arriving soon" — on the false premise that no component owned it. The "Where" column names the owner so that cannot recur. Governed by CHROME_EXEMPT in token-graph.ts; every entry states what a spoke loses. ${CHROME_EXEMPT.map((c) => `${c.prefix}* → ${c.owner}: ${c.cost}`).join(' ')}`, severity: 'info' },
        { id: 'adhoc', label: 'Ad-hoc hooks — promotion candidates', rows: adHocHooks, note: 'Declared in no token file, but always read WITH a fallback. Legitimate per SPEC.md: the component offers the hook via its fallback only. This is the working list for tier-3 surface work — promote into component-tokens.css, or fold away.', severity: 'info' },
        { id: 'unused', label: 'Unused ramp steps (tier 1)', rows: unusedRampSteps, note: 'Primitive steps nothing references. Normal — a 12-step scale is a palette, not a checklist. Useful only for spotting a whole ramp that is carried but never used.', severity: 'info' },
      ].map((check) => (
        <details class={`check ${check.severity}`} open={check.rows.length > 0 && check.severity !== 'info'}>
          <summary>
            <span class="check-label">{check.label}</span>
            <span class="check-count">{check.rows.length}</span>
          </summary>
          <p class="check-note">{check.note}</p>
          {check.rows.length === 0 ? (
            <p class="empty">None.</p>
          ) : (
            <table class="findings">
              <thead><tr><th>Token</th><th>Detail</th><th>Where</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                {check.rows.map((r) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td><code>{r.token}</code></td>
                    <td>{r.detail}</td>
                    <td class="dim">{r.where ?? '—'}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          )}
        </details>
      ))}
    </Section>

    <Section id="tier2-naming" title="Tier 2 · Naming audit">
      <p>
        Every tier-2 name measured against the 6-slot rubric. Counts here are derived from the real
        token names at build time, not typed in — the vocabulary tables and the prose are authored,
        because that part is judgment a parser can&rsquo;t make.
      </p>

      <div class="grammar">
        <div>
          <span class="grammar-label">Rubric</span>
          <code>{t2.grammars.rubric}</code>
        </div>
        <div>
          <span class="grammar-label">What we ship</span>
          {t2.grammars.current.map((g) => <code>{g}</code>)}
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="cat-note">
        Three slots where the rubric specifies six, and in the colour family slots 4 and 5 swap order
        depending on the token. The gap is front-loaded: everything from <em>variant</em> rightward is
        largely sound; everything to its left is missing or unstable.
      </p>

      <h3 class="sub">Slot by slot</h3>
      <table class="findings slots">
        <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Slot</th><th>Rubric</th><th>Where we are</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {t2.slots.map((s) => (
            <tr class={`slot-${s.status}`}>
              <td class="slot-n">{s.n}</td>
              <td class="slot-name">
                {s.name}
                <span class={`slot-flag f-${s.status}`}>{s.status}</span>
              </td>
              <td><code class="spec">{s.spec}</code></td>
              <td>{s.verdict}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3 class="sub">Slots 1–2 · no namespace, no tier signal</h3>
      <p class="cat-note">
        <b>{t2.prefixCollision.primitive}</b> tier-1 and <b>{t2.prefixCollision.semantic}</b> tier-2
        tokens share the <code>--color-</code> prefix. <code>{t2.prefixCollision.example.primitive}</code>
        and <code>{t2.prefixCollision.example.semantic}</code> are indistinguishable by name — under the
        rubric the second would be <code>--eco-theme-color-background-brand</code>.
      </p>
      <p class="cat-gap">
        <b>This has already cost you.</b> <code>token-graph.ts</code> classifies tier by
        <em>source directory</em> because no regex on the name can do it (<code>theming.ts</code> still
        tries the regex route). Two further costs have since been paid off. Both themes used to
        re-point <code>--radius-200</code>, a primitive — invisible, because nothing in the name
        announces which tier you are editing; they now re-point <code>--radius-surface</code>, the
        tier-2 role. And <code>--focus-ring-color</code> was once declared in both
        <code>tokens/primitive/effect.json</code> and <code>src/component-tokens.css</code>, with
        tier 3 loading last and winning; the tier-1 block was removed, so the ring lives only at
        tier 3 now. Both read <b>0</b> under <b>Health</b> above — but the prefix collision that let
        them happen unnoticed is still here, so the next one lands the same way.
      </p>

      <h3 class="sub">Slot 4 · the surface — biggest divergence</h3>
      <div class="tally">
        <div class="tally-cell t2"><b>{t2.surfaceNamed}</b><span>of {t2.colorTotal} name a surface</span></div>
        <div class="tally-cell warn"><b>{t2.surfaceImplied}</b><span>surface implied only</span></div>
        <div class="tally-cell ok"><b>{t2.orderRubric}</b><span>rubric order</span></div>
        <div class="tally-cell warn"><b>{t2.orderInverted}</b><span>inverted order</span></div>
        <div class="tally-cell"><b>{t2.orderSurfaceOnly}</b><span>surface, no variant</span></div>
        <div class="tally-cell warn"><b>{t2.variantAsSurface}</b><span>surface as a variant word</span></div>
      </div>

      <p class="cat-note">Five words in use for the rubric&rsquo;s three surfaces:</p>
      <table class="findings">
        <thead><tr><th>Rubric surface</th><th>Words in use</th><th>Tokens</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {t2.surfaceVocabRows.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td><code>{r.rubric}</code></td>
              <td>{r.words.map((w) => <code class="vocab">{w}</code>)}</td>
              <td class="num">{r.count}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p class="cat-note">
        <code>text</code> is a legal split of <code>content</code> per the rubric — but there is no
        <code>icon</code> counterpart, so icon colour is unmanaged at tier 2. And two suffixes are
        surfaces wearing a variant costume: <code>-strong</code> means <em>content</em> colour,
        <code>-on-fill</code> means <em>content on that background</em>.
      </p>

      <details class="tok-group" data-group>
        <summary>
          <code class="group-label">Every colour token, parsed into slots</code>
          <span class="summary-right"><span class="group-count">{t2.colorTotal}</span></span>
        </summary>
        <table class="tok-table parse">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Token</th><th>Surface</th><th>Variant</th><th>State</th><th>Order</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {t2.colorRows.map((r) => (
              <tr data-token={r.name}>
                <td class="c-name"><code>{r.name}</code></td>
                <td>
                  {r.surfaceWord ? (
                    <>
                      <code class="vocab">{r.surfaceWord}</code>
                      {r.surfaceWord !== r.surface && <span class="dim"> → {r.surface}</span>}
                    </>
                  ) : (
                    <span class="dim">implied</span>
                  )}
                </td>
                <td>{r.variant ? <code class="vocab">{r.variant}</code> : <span class="dim">—</span>}</td>
                <td>{r.state ? <code class="vocab st">{r.state}</code> : <span class="dim">—</span>}</td>
                <td><span class={`ord o-${r.order}`}>{r.order}</span></td>
                <td class="notes">
                  {r.flags.length === 0 ? <span class="dim">—</span> : r.flags.map((f) => <div>{f}</div>)}
                </td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </details>

      <h3 class="sub">Slot 5 · variant — right idea, different words</h3>
      <table class="findings">
        <thead><tr><th>Rubric</th><th>Current</th><th></th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {t2.variantVocab.map((v) => (
            <tr>
              <td><code>{v.rubric}</code></td>
              <td><code>{v.current}</code></td>
              <td><span class={`slot-flag f-${v.status}`}>{v.status}</span></td>
              <td>{v.note}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3 class="sub">Slot 6 · state — this one is already correct</h3>
      <p class="cat-note">
        All {t2.stateRows.length} stateful tokens put the state last, and <code>default</code> is
        correctly omitted rather than spelled out.
      </p>
      <table class="findings">
        <thead><tr><th>State</th><th>Tokens</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {t2.stateBreakdown.map((s) => (
            <tr>
              <td><code class="vocab st">{s.state}</code> <span class="num">({s.tokens.length})</span></td>
              <td>{s.tokens.map((n) => <code class="vocab">{n}</code>)}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3 class="sub">Slot 3 · which categories exist at tier 2 at all</h3>
      <table class="findings">
        <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Tokens</th><th>How it is handled today</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {t2.categoryRows.map((c) => (
            <tr class={c.count === 0 ? 'row-zero' : undefined}>
              <td><code>{c.category}</code></td>
              <td class="num">{c.count}</td>
              <td>{c.how}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p class="cat-note">
        Typography was the interesting one: the intention vocabulary existed only as CSS classes in
        <code>typography.css</code>, which Figma cannot consume and a spoke cannot re-point by
        overriding a token. It is now tokenised &mdash; {t2.nonColorGroups.find((g) => g.label === 'typography')?.tokens.length ?? 0} composite
        tokens assembled from the faces and weights below them, with the classes reading the
        composites rather than the primitives. The mapping the audit was built to measure:
      </p>
      <table class="findings">
        <thead><tr><th>Rubric intention</th><th>Current</th><th></th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {t2.compositeMapping.map((m) => (
            <tr>
              <td><code>{m.rubric}</code></td>
              <td><code>{m.current}</code></td>
              <td><span class={`slot-flag f-${m.status}`}>{m.status}</span></td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="cat-note">
        All {t2.compositeClasses.length} classes as shipped:
        {t2.compositeClasses.map((c) => <code class="vocab">.{c}</code>)}
      </p>

      <h3 class="sub">The non-colour tier-2 tokens</h3>
      <p class="cat-note">
        Colour is the only family the property-first rubric was written for. These are
        judged on the same question — does the name say which CSS declaration it belongs
        in — one row per family, because that is the level the claim is true at.
      </p>
      <table class="findings">
        <thead><tr><th>Family</th><th>Count</th><th>Names lead with</th><th>Property slot</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {t2.nonColorLeadRows().map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td><code>{r.label}</code></td>
              <td class="dim">{r.count}</td>
              <td class="dim">{r.leads}</td>
              <td>
                {r.property === 'first' ? <code class="vocab">first</code>
                : r.property === 'last' ? <code class="vocab warnv">last</code>
                : <code class="vocab warnv">none</code>}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </Section>

    <Section id="tier3-naming" title="Tier 3 · Naming audit">
      <p>
        The same exercise, one tier down. Every tier-3 name measured against the rubric&rsquo;s six
        slots, with counts derived from the real names at build time. Two things make tier 3 read
        differently from tier 2: the component slot is the one place this system is already
        <em>right</em>, and the names carry a seventh concept the rubric has no slot for.
      </p>

      <div class="grammar">
        <div>
          <span class="grammar-label">Rubric</span>
          <code>{t3.grammars.rubric}</code>
        </div>
        <div>
          <span class="grammar-label">What we ship</span>
          {t3.grammars.current.map((g) => <code>{g}</code>)}
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="tally">
        <div class="tally-cell t3"><b>{t3.total}</b><span>tier-3 tokens</span></div>
        <div class="tally-cell"><b>{t3.namespaceCount}</b><span>namespaces</span></div>
        <div class="tally-cell ok"><b>{t3.total - t3.unnamespaced.length}</b><span>name a component</span></div>
        <div class="tally-cell warn"><b>{t3.partTally.tokens}</b><span>name an internal part</span></div>
        <div class="tally-cell"><b>{t3.stateRows.length}</b><span>stateful</span></div>
        <div class="tally-cell warn"><b>{t3.unnamespaced.length}</b><span>unplaced</span></div>
      </div>

      <p class="cat-note">
        The shape of the gap is the mirror image of tier 2. There, everything left of
        <em>variant</em> was missing. Here slot 3 is solid and slots 4–5 are inverted: we write
        <code>--form-radius-md</code> where the rubric writes
        <code>--eco-component-form-md-radius</code>. Same information, opposite order.
      </p>

      <h3 class="sub">Slot by slot</h3>
      <table class="findings slots">
        <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Slot</th><th>Rubric</th><th>Where we are</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {t3.slots.map((s) => (
            <tr class={`slot-${s.status}`}>
              <td class="slot-n">{s.n}</td>
              <td class="slot-name">
                {s.name}
                <span class={`slot-flag f-${s.status}`}>{s.status}</span>
              </td>
              <td><code class="spec">{s.spec}</code></td>
              <td>{s.verdict}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3 class="sub">Slots 1–2 · where the missing tier identifier finally bites</h3>
      <p class="cat-note">
        At tier 2 the absent tier identifier was an argument about readability. At tier 3 it names
        real collisions — the same prefix declaring tokens at two tiers, with nothing in the name to
        say which one you are editing.
      </p>
      <table class="findings">
        <thead><tr><th>Prefix</th><th>At tier 1</th><th>At tier 3</th><th>Consequence</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {t3.tierCollisions.map((c) => (
            <tr class={c.live ? undefined : 'row-zero'}>
              <td>
                <code>{c.prefix}</code>
                <span class={`slot-flag f-${c.live ? 'absent' : 'match'}`}>{c.live ? 'live' : 'closed'}</span>
              </td>
              <td>{c.tier1.length ? c.tier1.map((n) => <code class="vocab">{n}</code>) : <span class="dim">none</span>}</td>
              <td>{c.tier3.length ? c.tier3.map((n) => <code class="vocab warnv">{n}</code>) : <span class="dim">none</span>}</td>
              <td>
                {c.cost}
                {c.fix && <div class="detail"><b>Closed:</b> {c.fix}</div>}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="cat-gap">
        <b>The rubric offers a cheaper fix here than at tier 2.</b> It says the tier identifier can be
        the <em>component prefix</em> itself — you don&rsquo;t need <code>--eco-component-</code> on all
        {t3.total} if the component name is doing that work. Which it already is, on
        {t3.total - t3.unnamespaced.length} of them. The remaining exposure is the
        {t3.unnamespaced.length} that name no component at all, plus the {t3.liveCollisions} prefix
        above that still straddles tiers.
      </p>
      <table class="findings">
        <thead><tr><th>Unplaced token</th><th>Declared under</th><th>Reads as</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {t3.unnamespaced.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td><code>{r.name}</code></td>
              <td><code class="vocab">{r.owner ?? '—'}</code></td>
              <td class="dim">
                {r.name.startsWith('--color-')
                  ? 'a tier-2 semantic colour role'
                  : 'a bare CSS property with no owner'}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3 class="sub">Slot 3 · component / category / special case — the one that already works</h3>
      <p class="cat-note">
        The rubric allows three kinds of name in this slot, and all three are in use. Reach is the
        number of distinct components that read a namespace&rsquo;s tokens — it is what separates a
        genuine category from a component that happens to be named oddly.
      </p>
      <table class="findings">
        <thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Namespaces</th><th>Tokens</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {t3.nsKindTally.map((k) => (
            <tr class={k.namespaces === 0 ? 'row-zero' : undefined}>
              <td>
                <code>{k.kind}</code>
                <span class={`slot-flag f-${k.kind === 'none' ? 'absent' : 'match'}`}>
                  {k.kind === 'none' ? 'unplaced' : 'rubric-legal'}
                </span>
              </td>
              <td class="num">{k.namespaces}</td>
              <td class="num">{k.tokens}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <table class="findings">
        <thead><tr><th>Namespace</th><th>Tokens</th><th>Reach</th><th>Why it is not a component name</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {t3.nsGroups.filter((g) => g.note).map((g) => (
            <tr>
              <td>
                <code>{g.ns}</code>
                <span class={`slot-flag f-${g.hasComponent ? 'match' : 'partial'}`}>{g.kind}</span>
              </td>
              <td class="num">{g.tokens.length}</td>
              <td class="num">{g.reach}</td>
              <td>{g.note}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="cat-note">
        <code>form</code> (read by {t3.nsGroups.find((g) => g.ns === 'form')?.reach} components) and
        <code>focus-ring</code> (read by {t3.nsGroups.find((g) => g.ns === 'focus-ring')?.reach}) are
        the rubric&rsquo;s own two examples, arrived at independently. Every other namespace has a
        reach of one or zero, which is what a component-scoped hook should look like.
      </p>
      <p class="cat-gap">
        <b>The boundary is not machine-readable.</b> Nothing in a name marks where the component ends
        and the property begins — these parse only because the parser checks the component roster
        longest-match-first:
        {t3.nsAmbiguity.map((p) => (
          <span class="pair"><code class="vocab">{p.shorter}</code> vs {p.longer.map((l) => <code class="vocab warnv">{l}</code>)}</span>
        ))}
        A dash-delimited slot the rubric assumes is one word is, here, one-to-three words that only a
        roster can segment.
      </p>
      {t3.ownerMismatch.length > 0 && (
        <>
          <p class="cat-note">
            {t3.ownerMismatch.length} tokens are named for one component but declared in another
            block. Mostly legitimate sharing — worth listing because it shows the block comment is
            not authoritative, the name is.
          </p>
          <table class="findings">
            <thead><tr><th>Token</th><th>Named for</th><th>Declared under</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {t3.ownerMismatch.map((r) => (
                <tr>
                  <td><code>{r.name}</code></td>
                  <td><code class="vocab">{r.ns}</code></td>
                  <td><code class="vocab warnv">{r.owner}</code></td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </>
      )}

      <h3 class="sub">The seventh concept · the internal part</h3>
      <p class="cat-note">
        <b>{t3.partTally.tokens} of {t3.total}</b> tokens name a piece <em>inside</em> the component —
        the row of a grid, the thumb of a switch, the header of a card — across
        {t3.partTally.distinct} distinct words. The rubric has six slots and none of them is this
        one. Under a strict reading these words land in <em>variant</em>, but a part is not a
        variant: <code>--grid-row-bg</code> and <code>--grid-header-bg</code> are not two variants of
        a grid, they are two different elements of the same one.
      </p>
      <p class="cat-gap">
        <b>Which matters because the slot is doing double duty.</b> The same unlabelled position
        holds parts ({t3.partTally.asPart} tokens), real variants ({t3.partTally.asVariant}) and even
        a state ({t3.partTally.asState}) — and nothing in the name tells you which you are reading.
        <code>--grid-row-bg</code> names a part, <code>--grid-row-stripe-bg</code> a variant of that
        part, and <code>--app-bar-brand-bg</code> a variant of the whole component. All three look
        the same.
      </p>
      {t3.partWordClash.length > 0 && (
        <p class="cat-gap">
          <b>And the same word can mean both.</b> {t3.partWordClash.map((c) => (
            <span class="pair">
              <code class="vocab warnv">{c.word}</code> is {c.readings.join(', ')}
            </span>
          ))} — <code>tone="brand"</code> on <code>esa-app-bar</code> against
          <code>.esa-app-shell__brand</code>, the BEM element. Two concepts, one spelling, one slot.
        </p>
      )}
      <details class="tok-group" data-group>
        <summary>
          <code class="group-label">Every word used in the part position</code>
          <span class="summary-right"><span class="group-count">{t3.partRows.length}</span></span>
        </summary>
        <table class="tok-table parse">
          <thead><tr><th>Word</th><th>Tokens</th><th>Actually a</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {t3.partRows.map((p) => (
              <tr>
                <td><code class="vocab">{p.part}</code></td>
                <td class="num">{p.count}</td>
                <td><span class={`pk pk-${p.kind}`}>{p.kind}</span></td>
                <td>{p.examples.map((e) => <code class="vocab">{e}</code>)}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </details>

      <h3 class="sub">Slot 4 · variant — present, and in the wrong place</h3>
      <div class="tally">
        <div class="tally-cell"><b>{t3.sizeRows.short.length + t3.sizeRows.long.length}</b><span>carry a size</span></div>
        <div class="tally-cell warn"><b>{t3.sizeRows.afterProperty.length}</b><span>of those, size after the property</span></div>
        <div class="tally-cell warn"><b>{t3.sizeRows.long.length}</b><span>in the long dialect</span></div>
        <div class="tally-cell"><b>{t3.variantRows.length}</b><span>semantic variants</span></div>
      </div>
      <p class="cat-note">
        The size axis that tier 2 skips entirely lands here instead, on
        {t3.sizeRows.scales.length} separate per-component scales — every one of them with the size
        trailing the property. <code>default</code> is correctly omitted throughout: there is no
        <code>--form-radius-default</code>, the unsuffixed name <em>is</em> the default.
      </p>
      <table class="findings">
        <thead><tr><th>Dialect</th><th>Tokens</th><th>Where</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code class="vocab">xs · sm · md · lg</code></td>
            <td class="num">{t3.sizeRows.short.length}</td>
            <td class="dim">The shared scale, aligned to Beacon&rsquo;s UiSize.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code class="vocab warnv">small · medium · large</code></td>
            <td class="num">{t3.sizeRows.long.length}</td>
            <td>{t3.sizeRows.long.map((r) => <code class="vocab">{r.name}</code>)}</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="cat-note">
        Semantic variants exist too, and they sit in the same trailing position:
        {t3.variantRows.map((r) => <code class="vocab">{r.name}</code>)}
      </p>

      <h3 class="sub">Slot 5 · property — nearly universal, never qualified</h3>
      <p class="cat-note">
        <b>{t3.withProperty} of {t3.total}</b> name a property. None of them qualify it the way the
        rubric does: <code>color-content</code>, <code>color-background</code> and
        <code>color-border</code> appear <b>{t3.colorQualified}</b> times in the whole file. Instead
        the colour-ness is implied and the word is abbreviated.
      </p>
      <table class="findings">
        <thead><tr><th>Rubric property</th><th>Words in use</th><th>Tokens</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {t3.propVocab.map((p) => (
            <tr>
              <td><code>{p.rubric}</code></td>
              <td>
                {p.words.map((w) => (
                  <code class={`vocab ${p.words.length > 1 ? 'warnv' : ''}`}>{w.word} <span class="num">{w.count}</span></code>
                ))}
              </td>
              <td class="num">{p.total}</td>
              <td>{p.note}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class={t3.borderSplit.shorthand.length ? 'cat-gap' : 'cat-note'}>
        {t3.borderSplit.shorthand.length ? (
          <>
            <b>One suffix, two property types.</b> <code>-border</code> holds a colour on
            {t3.borderSplit.colour.length} tokens and a full shorthand on {t3.borderSplit.shorthand.length}.
            Overriding the first changes a colour; overriding the second means restating the width
            and style to change one.
          </>
        ) : (
          <>
            <b>Closed.</b> <code>-border</code> used to name two different property types —
            a colour on <code>--sidenav-border</code> and <code>--topbar-border</code>, a whole
            <code>1px solid …</code> shorthand on <code>--filter-dropdown-border</code> — so two of
            the three were re-pointable and the third was not. All three are now
            <code>-border-color</code> holding a colour, and esa-filter-dropdown composes the
            shorthand from <code>--border-width-default</code>. The table below is derived, so it
            repopulates the moment a shorthand reappears.
          </>
        )}
      </p>
      <table class="findings">
        <thead><tr><th>Token</th><th>Holds</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {[...t3.borderSplit.colour, ...t3.borderSplit.shorthand].map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td><code>{r.name}</code></td>
              <td>
                <span class={`slot-flag f-${r.prop === 'color-border' ? 'match' : 'partial'}`}>
                  {r.prop === 'color-border' ? 'colour' : 'shorthand'}
                </span>
              </td>
              <td><code>{r.value}</code></td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      {t3.withoutProperty.length > 0 && (
        <p class="cat-note">
          {t3.withoutProperty.length} name no property at all — you have to read the value to know
          what they control:
          {t3.withoutProperty.map((r) => <code class="vocab warnv">{r.name}</code>)}
        </p>
      )}

      <h3 class="sub">Slot 6 · state — correctly placed, one word short</h3>
      <p class="cat-note">
        All {t3.stateRows.length} stateful tokens put the state last and omit
        <code>default</code>, exactly as the rubric prescribes.
        {t3.stateNotTrailing.length > 0 && (
          <>
            {' '}The exceptions are {t3.stateNotTrailing.length} tokens carrying a state word in the
            middle of the name instead:
            {t3.stateNotTrailing.map((r) => <code class="vocab warnv">{r.name}</code>)}
          </>
        )}
      </p>
      <table class="findings">
        <thead><tr><th>State</th><th>Tokens</th><th>Where we are</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {t3.stateVocab.map((s) => (
            <tr class={s.tokens.length === 0 ? 'row-zero' : undefined}>
              <td>
                <code class="vocab st">{s.state}</code>
                <span class={`slot-flag f-${s.inRubric ? 'match' : 'extra'}`}>
                  {s.inRubric ? 'rubric' : 'ours'}
                </span>
              </td>
              <td class="num">{s.tokens.length}</td>
              <td>
                {s.note && <div class="detail">{s.note}</div>}
                {s.tokens.map((n) => <code class="vocab">{n}</code>)}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="cat-note">
        <b>State is exclusive to colour — nearly.</b> {t3.stateColorCheck.onColor} of
        {t3.stateColorCheck.total} stateful tokens name a colour property outright. Of the
        {t3.stateColorCheck.exceptions.length} that don&rsquo;t, {t3.stateColorCheck.nonColour} really
        do move something other than a colour, and {t3.stateColorCheck.unnamedColour} is a colour
        whose property the name never states — so the rule holds on
        {t3.stateColorCheck.onColor + t3.stateColorCheck.unnamedColour} of {t3.stateColorCheck.total}.
      </p>
      <table class="findings">
        <thead><tr><th>Token</th><th>Property</th><th>State</th><th>Value</th><th></th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {t3.stateColorCheck.exceptions.map((e) => (
            <tr>
              <td><code>{e.row.name}</code></td>
              <td><code class="vocab warnv">{e.row.propWord ?? 'implied'}</code></td>
              <td><code class="vocab st">{e.row.state}</code></td>
              <td><code>{e.row.value}</code></td>
              <td>
                <span class={`slot-flag f-${e.kind === 'non-colour' ? 'absent' : 'partial'}`}>
                  {e.kind === 'non-colour' ? 'genuinely not a colour' : 'a colour, unnamed'}
                </span>
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3 class="sub">Colour · the narrower rubric</h3>
      <p class="cat-note">
        Colour gets its own shape, and it is stricter in two ways: the property is one of
        <em>three</em> rather than any CSS property, and <code>disabled</code> sits in the state slot
        alongside hover and focus. Measured against the {t3.colourTally.total} tier-3 tokens that
        actually hold a colour — a <code>1px solid …</code> shorthand and an
        <code>--elevation-*</code> shadow are both excluded, because neither is a name for a colour.
      </p>

      <div class="grammar">
        <div>
          <span class="grammar-label">Rubric</span>
          <code>{t3.colourGrammars.rubric}</code>
        </div>
        <div>
          <span class="grammar-label">What we ship</span>
          <code>{t3.colourGrammars.current}</code>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="tally">
        <div class="tally-cell t3"><b>{t3.colourTally.total}</b><span>colour tokens</span></div>
        <div class="tally-cell warn"><b>{t3.colourTally.qualified}</b><span>qualified <code>color-</code></span></div>
        <div class="tally-cell"><b>{t3.colourTally.background}</b><span>background</span></div>
        <div class="tally-cell"><b>{t3.colourTally.content}</b><span>content</span></div>
        <div class="tally-cell"><b>{t3.colourTally.border}</b><span>border</span></div>
        <div class="tally-cell warn"><b>{t3.colourTally.varianted}</b><span>carry a variant</span></div>
      </div>

      <table class="findings slots">
        <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Slot</th><th>Rubric</th><th>Where we are</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {t3.colourSlots.map((s) => (
            <tr class={`slot-${s.status}`}>
              <td class="slot-n">{s.n}</td>
              <td class="slot-name">
                {s.name}
                <span class={`slot-flag f-${s.status}`}>{s.status}</span>
              </td>
              <td><code class="spec">{s.spec}</code></td>
              <td>{s.verdict}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h4 class="sub2">The property, in {t3.colourPropVocab.reduce((n, p) => n + p.words.length, 0)} words instead of 3</h4>
      <table class="findings">
        <thead><tr><th>Rubric property</th><th>Our words</th><th>Tokens</th><th>Where we are</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {t3.colourPropVocab.map((p) => (
            <tr>
              <td><code class="spec">{p.rubric}</code></td>
              <td>
                {p.words.map((w) => (
                  <code class={`vocab ${p.words.length > 1 ? 'warnv' : ''}`}>{w.word} <span class="dim">×{w.count}</span></code>
                ))}
              </td>
              <td class="num">{p.total}</td>
              <td>{p.note}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      {t3.colourUnnamedProp.length > 0 && (
        <p class="cat-gap">
          <b>{t3.colourUnnamedProp.length} colour tokens name no property at all</b> — the value is the
          only way to know what they paint:
          {t3.colourUnnamedProp.map((r) => <code class="vocab warnv">{r.name}</code>)}
        </p>
      )}

      <h4 class="sub2">The variant slot · {t3.axisUnexposed.length} of {t3.intentionAxis.length} axes unexposed</h4>
      <p class="cat-note">
        The rubric&rsquo;s worked example is a button with primary / secondary / tertiary variants, each
        with its own background and its own states. So the real test is not whether any token carries a
        variant word — it is whether a component that <em>has</em> a colour-variant axis can be themed
        along it. <b>Intentions</b> is derived from the component source (which tier-2 roles the file
        reads); <b>varianted hooks</b> from the token names.
      </p>
      <table class="findings">
        <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Variant axis</th><th>Colour hooks</th><th>…varianted</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {t3.intentionAxis.map((a) => (
            <tr>
              <td><code>esa-{a.component}</code></td>
              <td>{a.intentions.map((i) => <code class="vocab">{i}</code>)}</td>
              <td class="num">{a.hooks}</td>
              <td class="num">
                {a.varianted === 0
                  ? <span class="slot-flag f-absent">none</span>
                  : a.varianted}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="cat-gap">
        <b>This is a cost, not a bug.</b> Those components express their variants by reading tier-2
        intentions directly, which is legal and keeps the surface small — the whole kit&rsquo;s danger
        moves as one. What it forecloses is the other direction: a spoke cannot make its danger
        <em>button</em> differ from its danger <em>badge</em>, because there is no hook between them.
        {t3.colourVariantInverted.length > 0 && (
          <>
            {' '}The {t3.colourVariantInverted.length} tokens that do carry a variant put it after the
            property, which is the rubric&rsquo;s order reversed:
            {t3.colourVariantInverted.map((r) => <code class="vocab warnv">{r.name}</code>)}
          </>
        )}
      </p>

      <h4 class="sub2">The state slot</h4>
      <table class="findings">
        <thead><tr><th>State</th><th>Tokens</th><th>Where we are</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {t3.colourStateVocab.map((s) => (
            <tr class={s.tokens.length === 0 ? 'row-zero' : undefined}>
              <td>
                <code class="vocab st">{s.state}</code>
                <span class={`slot-flag f-${s.inRubric ? 'match' : 'extra'}`}>
                  {s.inRubric ? 'rubric' : 'ours'}
                </span>
              </td>
              <td class="num">{s.tokens.length}</td>
              <td>
                {s.note && <div class="detail">{s.note}</div>}
                {s.tokens.map((n) => <code class="vocab">{n}</code>)}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <details class="tok-group" data-group>
        <summary>
          <code class="group-label">Every tier-3 token, parsed into slots</code>
          <span class="summary-right"><span class="group-count">{t3.total}</span></span>
        </summary>
        <table class="tok-table parse">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Token</th><th>Component</th><th>Part</th><th>Variant</th>
              <th>Property</th><th>State</th><th>Notes</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {t3.rows.map((r) => (
              <tr data-token={r.name}>
                <td class="c-name"><code>{r.name}</code></td>
                <td>
                  {r.ns ? (
                    <>
                      <code class="vocab">{r.ns}</code>
                      {r.nsKind !== 'component' && <span class="dim"> {r.nsKind}</span>}
                    </>
                  ) : (
                    <span class="ord o-none">unplaced</span>
                  )}
                </td>
                <td>{r.part ? <code class="vocab">{r.part}</code> : <span class="dim">—</span>}</td>
                <td>
                  {r.size || r.variant ? (
                    <code class="vocab">{[r.variant, r.size].filter(Boolean).join(' · ')}</code>
                  ) : (
                    <span class="dim">—</span>
                  )}
                </td>
                <td>
                  {r.propWord ? (
                    <>
                      <code class="vocab">{r.propWord}</code>
                      {r.prop !== 'other' && r.prop !== null && (
                        <span class="dim"> → {r.prop}</span>
                      )}
                    </>
                  ) : (
                    <span class="dim">implied</span>
                  )}
                </td>
                <td>{r.state ? <code class="vocab st">{r.state}</code> : <span class="dim">—</span>}</td>
                <td class="notes">
                  {r.flags.length === 0 ? <span class="dim">—</span> : r.flags.map((f) => <div>{f}</div>)}
                </td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </details>

      <h3 class="sub">Typography · the third narrow rubric</h3>
      <p class="cat-note">
        Colour is not the only family with its own shape. Typography gets one too —
        <code class="spec">--&lt;component&gt;-&lt;variant&gt;-&lt;font-size|font-weight|font-style|line-height&gt;</code>
        — and it differs from colour in the direction it wants to be measured. The model is explicit
        that components should wire to the tier-2 typography <em>composites</em>, and that tier 3 is
        for the exceptions only: <q>most of the use cases for this are either tweaking a font size, or
        setting a line height to one in order to remove white space above and below a text</q>. So a
        <em>small</em> number here is the healthy result. A large tier-3 type surface would mean
        components are re-deriving type instead of reading a role.
      </p>
      <div class="tally">
        <div class="tally-cell t3"><b>{t3.typeTally.total}</b><span>typographic tier-3 tokens</span></div>
        <div class="tally-cell ok"><b>{t3.typeTally.rubricSpelled}</b><span>spelled the rubric&rsquo;s way</span></div>
        <div class="tally-cell warn"><b>{t3.typeTally.total - t3.typeTally.rubricSpelled - t3.typeTally.outside}</b><span>abbreviated</span></div>
        <div class="tally-cell warn"><b>{t3.typeTally.outside}</b><span>property outside the four</span></div>
      </div>
      <table class="findings">
        <thead><tr><th>Rubric property</th><th>Our words</th><th>Tokens</th><th>Where we are</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {t3.typePropVocab.map((p) => (
            <tr>
              <td><code class="spec">{p.rubric}</code></td>
              <td>
                {p.words.length === 0 ? <span class="dim">—</span> : p.words.map((w) => (
                  <code class={`vocab ${p.words.length > 1 ? 'warnv' : ''}`}>{w.word} <span class="dim">×{w.count}</span></code>
                ))}
              </td>
              <td class="num">{p.total}</td>
              <td>{p.note}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      {t3.typeOutsideRubric.length > 0 && (
        <p class="cat-gap">
          <b>{t3.typeOutsideRubric.length} outside the model&rsquo;s four properties</b> — the rubric closes
          the list at font-size, font-weight, font-style and line-height, so these have no slot to sit in:
          {t3.typeOutsideRubric.map((t) => <code class="vocab warnv">{t.row.name}</code>)}
        </p>
      )}
    </Section>

    <Section id="tier1-naming" title="Tier 1 · Naming audit">
      <p>
        The last of the three, and the one with the most riding on it. A tier-1 name is the only
        description a raw value ever gets — there is no layer beneath it to explain what
        <code>--color-yellow-9</code> or <code>--shadow-blur-200</code> means. Counts are derived from the
        real names and the real files at build time; the prose is authored.
      </p>

      <div class="grammar">
        <div>
          <span class="grammar-label">Rubric</span>
          <code>{t1.grammars.rubric}</code>
        </div>
        <div>
          <span class="grammar-label">What we ship</span>
          {t1.grammars.current.map((g) => <code>{g}</code>)}
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="tally">
        <div class="tally-cell t1"><b>{t1.total}</b><span>tier-1 tokens</span></div>
        <div class="tally-cell"><b>{t1.rampTally.ramps}</b><span>colour ramps</span></div>
        <div class="tally-cell ok"><b>{t1.rampTally.used}</b><span>of {t1.rampTally.steps} ramp steps read</span></div>
        <div class="tally-cell warn"><b>{t1.aliasRows.length}</b><span>aliases, not primitives</span></div>
        <div class="tally-cell warn"><b>{t1.straddleRows.length}</b><span>prefixes straddling tiers</span></div>
        <div class="tally-cell warn"><b>{t1.ghostRows.length}</b><span>retired names still cited</span></div>
      </div>

      <p class="cat-note">
        The shape of the gap is different again. Tier 2 was missing everything left of
        <em>variant</em>; tier 3 had the object slot right and the modifiers inverted. Tier 1 gets its
        <em>scales</em> almost entirely right — the Radix 12-step ramp is the best-named thing in the
        system — and then packs three unrelated concepts into one slot.
      </p>
      <p class="cat-note">
        <b>{t1.worklistDone} of {t1.worklist.length} findings are closed.</b> Every count on this page
        is re-derived at build time, so a section that reads &ldquo;resolved&rdquo; is reporting that
        its own check now returns zero — not that someone edited a list. The findings stay on the page
        after they close: what was found and why it mattered is the part worth keeping.
      </p>

      <h3 class="sub">Slot by slot</h3>
      <table class="findings slots">
        <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Slot</th><th>Rubric</th><th>Where we are</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {t1.slots.map((s) => (
            <tr class={`slot-${s.status}`}>
              <td class="slot-n">{s.n}</td>
              <td class="slot-name">
                {s.name}
                <span class={`slot-flag f-${s.status}`}>{s.status}</span>
              </td>
              <td><code class="spec">{s.spec}</code></td>
              <td>{s.verdict}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3 class="sub">Slots 1–2 · where the missing tier identifier is load-bearing</h3>
      <p class="cat-note">
        SPEC.md&rsquo;s central rule is that a component reads tier 2 and never a primitive, and it
        names <code>border-radius: var(--radius-200)</code> as <em>the</em> bug. These are the
        prefixes where that bug is invisible in a diff, because the tier-1 and tier-2 names are the
        same shape.
      </p>
      <table class="findings">
        <thead><tr><th>Prefix</th><th>Tier 1</th><th>Tier 2</th><th>Tier 3</th><th>What it costs</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {t1.straddleRows.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td><code>--{r.prefix}-</code></td>
              <td class="num">{r.tier1.length}</td>
              <td class="num">{r.tier2.length || <span class="dim">—</span>}</td>
              <td class="num">{r.tier3.length || <span class="dim">—</span>}</td>
              <td class="detail">{r.cost}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3 class="sub">Not primitives · {t1.aliasRows.length} tier-1 tokens whose value is a reference</h3>
      <p class="cat-note">
        A primitive holds a value. A token holding a <em>reference</em> is an alias, and an alias is
        an intent expressed in terms of something else — tier 2&rsquo;s job. These are found
        structurally (the compiled value is a bare <code>var()</code>), not by recognising names, so a
        new one cannot slip in unnoticed.
      </p>
      {t1.aliasRows.length > 0 ? (
        <table class="findings">
          <thead><tr><th>Token</th><th>Points at</th><th>Rubric levels in the name</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {t1.aliasRows.map((r) => (
              <tr>
                <td><code>{r.name}</code></td>
                <td><code class="vocab">{r.value}</code></td>
                <td class="dim">{r.levels}</td>
                <td class="detail">{r.verdict}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      ) : (
        <p class="resolved">
          <span class="slot-flag f-match">resolved</span>
          The check returns zero. It found 14: the 12 <code>--color-status-*</code> tokens plus
          <code>--typography-font-family-display</code> and <code>--font-family-primary</code>. The status family turned
          out not to need moving at all — the tier-2 roles already existed and merely detoured through
          it, so they were repointed straight at their ramp steps and the block deleted.
          <code>--font-family-primary</code> went with it (zero readers);
          <code>--typography-font-family-display</code> moved to <code>semantic/typography.json</code> keeping its name,
          so every reader was untouched.
        </p>
      )}
      <p class="cat-gap">
        <b>Why this was not cosmetic.</b> SPEC.md&rsquo;s rule is that primitives never move — not in
        the hub, not in a theme. Declaring the status family in
        <code>tokens/primitive/color.json</code> made it unthemeable: a spoke that needed different
        status hues had no legal override anywhere in the system. The tell that it was a
        <em>split</em> rather than a style choice was that one member had already migrated —
        <code>danger</code> was a tier-2 role pointing straight at <code>red.9</code>, while its three
        siblings detoured through tier 1. The table below is the same check today.
      </p>
      <table class="findings">
        <thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>At tier 1</th><th>At tier 2</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {t1.conceptSplit.map((c) => (
            <tr class={c.tier1.length && c.tier2.length ? undefined : 'row-zero'}>
              <td><code>{c.concept}</code></td>
              <td>
                {c.tier1.length
                  ? c.tier1.map((n) => <code class="vocab warnv">{n}</code>)
                  : <span class="dim">none</span>}
              </td>
              <td>
                {c.tier2.length
                  ? c.tier2.map((n) => <code class="vocab">{n}</code>)
                  : <span class="dim">none</span>}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3 class="sub">
        Slot 3 · typography, by CSS property ({t1.typeTally.covered} of {t1.typeTally.properties})
      </h3>
      <div class="grammar">
        <div>
          <span class="grammar-label">Convention adopted</span>
          <code>--&lt;css-property&gt;-&lt;value&gt;</code>
        </div>
        <div>
          <span class="grammar-label">Previously</span>
          <code>--font-&lt;variant&gt; · --type-size-&lt;step&gt; · --line-height-&lt;name&gt;</code>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="cat-note">
        Typography used to be where the category slot fell apart — five name shapes for one category,
        including a second category word. It resolved by dropping the category instead of inventing
        one: the token name is now a real CSS property plus the value it holds, so it needs no
        grouping word and it reads directly as the declaration it will end up in. All
        {' '}<b>{t1.typeTally.conformant}</b> tier-1 type tokens follow it. Two rules are being
        measured — the name leads with a real property, and the <em>value</em> segment describes the
        value rather than a job.
      </p>
      <table class="findings">
        <thead><tr><th>CSS property</th><th>Value naming</th><th>Values</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {t1.typeProperties.map((r) => (
            <tr class={r.members.length ? `slot-${r.status}` : 'slot-absent'}>
              <td>
                <code class="spec">--{r.property}-*</code>
                <span class={`slot-flag f-${r.members.length ? r.status : 'absent'}`}>
                  {r.members.length ? r.status : 'no tokens'}
                </span>
              </td>
              <td class="dim">{r.valueStyle}</td>
              <td>
                {r.values.length
                  ? r.values.map((v) => <code class="vocab">{v}</code>)
                  : <span class="dim">—</span>}
              </td>
              <td class="detail">{r.note}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p class="cat-gap">
        <b>The split that made it work.</b> The rule that tier-1 names describe values is what forced
        the roles out. <code>--typography-font-family-sans</code> names a classification, not a face, and
        <code>--typography-font-weight-regular: 350</code> names a job, not a weight — yet
        <code>typography.css</code> documented both as the surface a spoke re-points, which made the
        shipped type contract an instruction to re-point a primitive. These are the roles, now at
        tier 2 with their names unchanged, so no reader had to move:
      </p>
      <table class="findings">
        <thead><tr><th>Tier-2 role</th><th>Points at</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {t1.composites.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td><code>{r.name}</code></td>
              <td><code class="vocab">{r.value}</code></td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3 class="sub">Slots 3–6 · one slot carrying hue, mode and channel</h3>
      <p class="cat-note">
        Everything between <code>--color-</code> and the step number is a single undifferentiated
        slot, and three different kinds of thing are living in it: a hue (<code>gray</code>), a hue
        plus a <em>mode</em> (<code>gray-dark</code>), and a hue plus an alpha <em>channel</em>
        (<code>gray-a</code>). The rubric appends mode last — <code>--color-gray-11-dark</code> — for
        exactly this reason.
      </p>
      <table class="findings">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Ramp</th><th>Slot holds</th><th>Hue</th><th>Modifier</th><th>Steps</th><th>With a reader</th><th>Name matches value</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {t1.rampRows.map((r) => (
            <tr class={r.used === 0 ? 'row-zero' : undefined}>
              <td><code>--color-{r.base}-*</code></td>
              <td><span class={`pk pk-${r.kind === 'hue' ? 'part' : 'variant'}`}>{r.kind}</span></td>
              <td class="dim">{r.hue}</td>
              <td>{r.modifier ? <code class="vocab warnv">-{r.modifier}</code> : <span class="dim">—</span>}</td>
              <td class="num">{r.steps}</td>
              <td class="num">{r.used}</td>
              <td>
                {r.renamedFrom
                  ? <span class="inf">no — this is Radix {r.renamedFrom}</span>
                  : <span class="dim">yes</span>}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="cat-gap">
        <b>Two things this table makes visible.</b> First, the {t1.rampTally.renamed} ramps whose name
        does not describe their value — both admit it in a <code>$description</code> that says
        &ldquo;named for backward compat&rdquo;, which is a note a designer cross-referencing the
        Radix docs will never read. Second, the {t1.rampTally.mode} mode ramps show zero readers here,
        and that is not rot: they are consumed by
        <code>{t1.darkModeConsumer.file}</code> ({t1.darkModeConsumer.refs} references), a
        <code>[data-scheme]</code> block this graph deliberately excludes. The naming is what forces
        that arrangement — a semantic role can hold one value per scope, so putting mode in the ramp
        name pushes dark mode out of the token package entirely and into app CSS a spoke does not get.
      </p>

      <h3 class="sub">
        Fallback drift · {t1.fallbackTally.tokens} tokens whose read sites disagree with them
      </h3>
      <p class="cat-note">
        CLAUDE.md requires every private token to carry a literal fallback —
        <code>var(--font-size-200, 0.875rem)</code>. Nothing checks that the literal agrees with the
        token. For a scale named for a <em>rung</em> rather than a value there is nothing for an
        author to check against, so they guess, and they guess differently:
        {' '}<b>{t1.fallbackTally.contradictory}</b> of these carry more than one distinct fallback
        across the kit. The <b>{t1.fallbackTally.wrong}</b> marked <em>exact</em> are the unambiguous
        ones — the token holds a single literal, so the fallback could have been right and isn&rsquo;t.
      </p>
      <p class="cat-gap">
        <b>This check is scoped, and the scope hides something.</b> It looks only at the tier-1
        measure tokens. Run the same comparison across <em>every</em> token and it returns roughly
        <b> 1,250</b> disagreeing read sites in ~110 files — overwhelmingly colour
        (<code>--color-content-default</code> is <code>#202020</code> and is written as
        <code>#171717</code> in 118 places; <code>--color-background-brand</code> is grass and is written as a
        navy left over from another brand in 72). That is a real finding and it is <em>not</em> being
        fixed here: it is mostly tier-2 colour, it needs its own pass, and a blind rewrite would be
        wrong in at least one class of case — <code>var(--typography-font-family-sans, sans-serif)</code> is a
        deliberate generic-family degradation, not a failed restatement of the token, and
        &ldquo;correcting&rdquo; it would name a face that by definition isn&rsquo;t loading.
      </p>
      <table class="findings">
        <thead><tr><th>Token</th><th>Actual value</th><th>Fallbacks written</th><th>Read in</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {t1.fallbackDrift.map((r) => (
            <tr class={r.exact ? 'slot-absent' : undefined}>
              <td>
                <code>{r.token}</code>
                {r.exact && <span class="slot-flag f-absent">exact</span>}
              </td>
              <td><code class="vocab">{r.actual}</code></td>
              <td>{r.fallbacks.map((f) => <code class="vocab warnv">{f}</code>)}</td>
              <td class="dim">{r.files.length} file{r.files.length === 1 ? '' : 's'}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3 class="sub">Slot 5 · the scale systems</h3>
      <p class="cat-note">
        Mixed scale <em>types</em> across categories is what the rubric asks for — &ldquo;homogeneity
        within a class, heterogeneity between classes.&rdquo; So most of this table is a pass. What
        matters is the family that breaks the convention inside its own class.
      </p>
      <table class="findings">
        <thead><tr><th>Family</th><th>Scale</th><th>Steps</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {t1.scaleRows.map((r) => (
            <tr class={r.ragged ? 'slot-absent' : undefined}>
              <td><code>{r.family}</code></td>
              <td>
                <span class={`slot-flag f-${r.ragged ? 'absent' : 'match'}`}>{r.kind}</span>
              </td>
              <td>
                {r.steps.map((s) => (
                  <code class={r.escapes.includes(s) ? 'vocab st' : 'vocab'}>{s}</code>
                ))}
              </td>
              <td class="detail">{r.note}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3 class="sub">Two names, one value</h3>
      <table class="findings">
        <thead><tr><th>Value</th><th>Names</th><th>Reading</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {t1.dupeRows.map((r) => (
            <tr class={r.kind === 'defect' ? 'slot-absent' : r.kind === 'by design' ? 'row-zero' : 'row-zero'}>
              <td><code class="vocab">{r.value}</code></td>
              <td>{r.names.map((n) => <code class="vocab">{n}</code>)}</td>
              <td class="detail">
                <span class={`slot-flag f-${r.kind === 'defect' ? 'absent' : 'match'}`}>{r.kind}</span>
                <div>{r.note}</div>
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p class="cat-note">
        The alpha ramps are the subtler case, and they are <em>not</em> duplicates. Both are black at
        an alpha, and they run two different progressions: <code>gray-a</code> follows Radix&rsquo;s
        perceptual curve, <code>black-a</code> a hand-set linear one. Nothing in either name says
        which you are asking for, and the source files don&rsquo;t agree on notation either —
        <code>gray-a</code> is authored as 8-digit hex
        (<code>{t1.alphaRamps.notation.grayA}</code>), <code>black-a</code> as
        <code>{t1.alphaRamps.notation.blackA}</code>. That last difference is invisible in the
        shipped CSS, because Style Dictionary normalises both to <code>rgba()</code> on the way out.
        <code>gray-a</code> has {t1.alphaRamps.grayAUsed} readers;
        <code>black-a</code> has {t1.alphaRamps.blackAUsed}.
      </p>
      <table class="findings">
        <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>--color-gray-a-*</th><th>α</th><th>--color-black-a-*</th><th>α</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {t1.alphaRamps.rows.map((r) => (
            <tr class={r.same ? 'slot-absent' : undefined}>
              <td class="num">{r.step}</td>
              <td><code>{r.grayA}</code></td>
              <td class="num">{r.grayAlpha}</td>
              <td><code>{r.blackA}</code></td>
              <td class="num">{r.blackAlpha}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3 class="sub">The rename that stopped at the package boundary</h3>
      <p class="cat-note">
        Tier 1 has been renamed once already: a <code>-50…900</code> lightness scale became the Radix
        <code>-1…12</code> step scale. The compiled output moved; the citations did not. This check
        looks for names matching the retired shape that are still read or documented anywhere in
        <code>packages/</code>, <code>apps/</code>, <code>plugins/</code> or the root docs, and that
        exist nowhere in the shipped tokens — the standing cost of a tier-1 rename when the names
        carry no version or tier signal to grep for.
      </p>
      {t1.ghostRows.length > 0 ? (
        <table class="findings">
          <thead><tr><th>Retired name</th><th>Still cited in</th><th>Impact</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {t1.ghostRows.map((g) => (
              <tr class="slot-absent">
                <td><code>{g.name}</code></td>
                <td>{g.files.map((f) => <code class="vocab warnv">{f}</code>)}</td>
                <td class="detail">{g.impact}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      ) : (
        <p class="resolved">
          <span class="slot-flag f-match">resolved</span>
          The check returns zero. It found 6 across 5 files. The live ones were in
          <code>packages/spoke-template/</code> — <code>--color-background-success</code>,
          <code>--color-background-warning</code> and <code>--color-background-ai</code> were <code>var()</code> reads of
          names that no longer existed, so every new spoke started with those properties dropped.
          The rest were documentation: SPEC.md, CLAUDE.md and README.md all taught
          <code>--color-teal-900</code>.
        </p>
      )}
      <p class="cat-gap">
        <b>Where they came from.</b> Two CSS files sat at the root of
        <code>packages/tokens/</code>, superseded by <code>src/</code> and <code>dist/</code> and in
        neither the package&rsquo;s <code>exports</code> nor its <code>files</code> — so nothing
        loaded them, which is precisely why nobody noticed they still described a different system.
        This table scans that directory; it is empty because both have been deleted.
      </p>
      {t1.strayFiles.length > 0 ? (
        <table class="findings">
          <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Declares</th><th>Names not in the shipped output</th><th>Reachable?</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {t1.strayFiles.map((f) => (
              <tr class={f.ghosts ? 'slot-absent' : undefined}>
                <td><code>{f.file}</code></td>
                <td class="num">{f.names}</td>
                <td class="num">{f.ghosts}</td>
                <td>
                  <span class={`slot-flag f-${f.exported || f.packed ? 'partial' : 'match'}`}>
                    {f.exported ? 'exported' : f.packed ? 'published' : 'dead'}
                  </span>
                </td>
                <td class="detail">{f.note}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      ) : (
        <p class="resolved">
          <span class="slot-flag f-match">resolved</span>
          No CSS files at the package root. <code>tokens.css</code> (157 declarations, 78 of them
          retired names) and <code>component-tokens.css</code> (31 / 13) are gone.
        </p>
      )}

      <h3 class="sub">Worklist</h3>
      <p class="cat-note">
        Ordered by cost-to-value, not by severity, and each row&rsquo;s <em>done</em> state comes from
        the same check that raised it — not from anyone editing this list. The three that are closed
        were all value-neutral: a rebuild of the pre-change sources against the current ones changed
        0 of 421 token values. What is left splits into one rename that gets more expensive with time
        (typography) and two decisions that should be made before any more renaming happens.
      </p>
      <table class="findings">
        <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Fix</th><th>Effort</th><th>Remaining</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {t1.worklist.map((f) => (
