    </Section>

    <Section id="component" title="Tier 3 · Component">
      <p>{TIER_NOTE.component}</p>
      {componentGroups.map((group: TokenGroup) => <TokenGroupTable group={group} />)}
    </Section>

    <Section id="semantic" title="Tier 2 · Semantic">
      <p>{TIER_NOTE.semantic}</p>
      <p class="cat-note">
        Grouped by <b>category</b> first &mdash; the word each name leads with &mdash; then, inside
        colour, by the property it lands on. This tree used to open on
        <code>background</code>/<code>content</code>/<code>border</code> with every non-colour family
        listed after them, which made one category&rsquo;s properties peers of whole categories and
        left <code>color</code> with no heading at all. The rubric&rsquo;s slot 3 above already asks
        which categories exist; this is the same question answered as a tree.
      </p>
      <p class="cat-note">
        Category here is the NAMESPACE, not the rubric&rsquo;s taxonomy: the rubric files border
        colours and border widths together under <code>border</code>, which is true but cannot be a
        tree &mdash; <code>--color-border-danger</code> would need to be in two places. One token,
        one node.{' '}
        <b>{t2.categoryCoverage.grouped}</b> of <b>{t2.categoryCoverage.total}</b> tier-2 tokens are
        filed below.
      </p>
      {t2.categoryCoverage.missing.length > 0 && (
        <p class="cat-gap">
          <b>{t2.categoryCoverage.missing.length} tokens are in no category</b> and therefore render
          nowhere on this page: {t2.categoryCoverage.missing.map((n) => <code class="vocab">{n}</code>)}.
          The tree is built from two independently-assembled halves (colour, and the non-colour
          families), so it can stop being a partition without any count on the page looking wrong.
          That is what this line exists to catch.
        </p>
      )}

      {t2.semanticCategories.map((cat) => (
        <details class="tok-category cat" data-group open={cat.kind === 'color'}>
          <summary>
            <span class="cat-label">{cat.label}</span>
            <span class="summary-right">
              {/* The catch-all is empty in the healthy case, so it carries no verdict —
                  a chip there would read as a finding about tokens that do not exist. */}
              {cat.kind === 'family' && !cat.isUnclassified && cat.property === 'last' && (
                <span class="gap-flag">property last</span>
              )}
              {cat.kind === 'family' && !cat.isUnclassified && cat.property === 'none' && (
                <span class="gap-flag">no property slot</span>
              )}
              <span class={cat.isUnclassified && cat.count === 0 ? 'group-count none' : 'group-count'}>
                {cat.count}
              </span>
            </span>
          </summary>
          <p class="cat-note">{cat.note}</p>

          {cat.kind === 'color' && (
          <div class="cat-body">
      <p class="cat-note">
        Colour tokens map tier 1 to specific colour roles in the default (vanilla) theme, grouped here
        by <b>property</b> — the surface the colour lands on — then by variant family within each.
        {t2.surfaceImplied === 0 ? (
          <>
            All {t2.colorTotal} spell their property in the name, so the grouping below is read off
            the names rather than inferred from convention. This was the point of the property-first
            rename: nothing here is placed by knowing where it goes.
          </>
        ) : (
          <>
            Only {t2.surfaceNamed} of {t2.colorTotal} spell their property in the name; the other
            {' '}{t2.surfaceImplied} are placed by convention and marked <span class="inf">inferred</span>,
            so this grouping doubles as the worklist for making the names say what the grouping knows.
          </>
        )}
      </p>

      {t2.propertyGroups.map((prop) => (
        <details class="tok-category prop" data-group open>
          <summary>
            <span class="cat-label">{prop.label}</span>
            <span class="summary-right">
              {prop.inferred > 0 && <span class="inf-flag">{prop.inferred} inferred</span>}
              <span class="group-count">{prop.total}</span>
            </span>
          </summary>
          <p class="cat-note">{prop.note}</p>
          <p class="cat-note">
            Spelled in the name as {prop.words.map((w) => <code class="vocab">{w}</code>)}
            {prop.words.length > 1 && <span> — {prop.words.length} words for one property.</span>}
            {' '}<b>{prop.declared}</b> declared · <b>{prop.inferred}</b> inferred.
          </p>
          <div class="cat-body">
            {prop.families.map((fam) => (
              <details class={fam.isUnclassified && fam.rows.length === 0 ? 'tok-group is-empty' : 'tok-group'} data-group>
                <summary>
                  <code class="group-label">{fam.label}</code>
                  <span class="summary-right">
                    <span class={fam.isUnclassified && fam.rows.length === 0 ? 'group-count none' : 'group-count'}>
                      {fam.rows.length}
                    </span>
                  </span>
                </summary>
                {fam.label === 'default' && (
                  <p class="cat-note">
                    <b>The tokens say this word now.</b> Ten of them were named without it &mdash;{' '}
                    <code>--color-background</code>, <code>--color-border</code>,{' '}
                    <code>--color-content-primary</code>, <code>--color-border-subtle</code> and the
                    rest &mdash; until 2026-08-15, relying on a convention of intention-by-omission
                    that only reads if you already know it. See <code>bare-property-to-default</code>
                    {' '}and <code>missing-intention-to-default</code> in{' '}
                    <code>migrations.json</code>. Everything here is the DEFAULT intention: the token
                    itself, its prominence variants and its states.
                  </p>
                )}
                {fam.isUnclassified && (
                  <p class="cat-note">
                    Nothing should be here, and that emptiness is the claim: every tier-2 colour name
                    fills slot 2 with a real intention. This grouping is
                    {' '}<code>property &gt; intention &gt; variant &gt; state</code>, and the label
                    on every group above is slot 2 alone. Earlier versions collapsed intention and
                    variant into one axis, which cannot be done &mdash; so each one had to invent an
                    umbrella (<code>neutral</code>, then <code>default</code>-as-a-bucket, then{' '}
                    <code>utility</code>) to stand in for the slot that had been deleted. Anything
                    whose slot 2 is not an intention lands here instead of being absorbed.
                  </p>
                )}
                {/* Scrolls inside its own box. Six columns of token names and
                    resolution chains do not fit a narrow viewport, and without this
                    the widest row pushed the whole page sideways. */}
                <div class="table-scroll">
                <table class="tok-table">
                  <thead>
                    {/* The four slots, in grammar order, then the value. `Property`
                        used to sit here and said the same word as the group heading
                        above — while `intention`, the slot the grouping keys on, had
                        no column at all. */}
                    <tr><th>Token</th><th>Intention</th><th>Variant</th><th>Knockout</th><th>State</th><th>Resolves to</th><th>Used by</th></tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody>
                    {fam.rows.map((r) => {
                      const node = t2.semanticNodes.get(r.name);
                      return (
                        <tr data-token={r.name} title={node?.description ?? undefined}>
                          <td class="c-name">
                            <code>{r.name}</code>
                            {/* One intention by NAME, another by VALUE. Not moved to the
                                other family: the name is not wrong, it is derived —
                                see --color-border-default-focus. */}
                            {r.derivedFrom && (
                              <span
                                class="derived"
                                title={`The name says ${r.intention ?? 'no intention'}, but the value chains through the ${r.derivedFrom} intention — so a spoke re-pointing ${r.derivedFrom} moves this too.`}
                              >via {r.derivedFrom}</span>
                            )}
                          </td>
                          <td>
                            {r.intention ? (
                              <>
                                {r.on && <span class="on-prefix" title="Relational: the foreground colour for that intention's fill. The grammar has no slot for it.">on</span>}
                                <code class="vocab">{r.intention}</code>
                              </>
                            ) : (
                              <span class="inf" title="Slot 2 is empty — the name carries a variant or a state with no intention in front of it.">not filled</span>
                            )}
                          </td>
                          <td>{r.variant ? <code class="vocab">{r.variant}</code> : <span class="dim">—</span>}</td>
                          {/* Its own column, not folded into Variant: the variant slot is
                              often already taken (`raised`), and joining them would give
                              the fused `raised-knockout` — the same defect as collapsing
                              intention into variant. A dash here on a neutral chrome role
                              is the audit: that role has no knocked-out counterpart, so a
                              component rendering it inside an inverted region falls back
                              to a value built for the opposite ground. */}
                          <td>{r.knockout ? <code class="vocab ko" title="This role against the REVERSE of the theme's ground. Not `the dark value` — a dark-default theme re-points this to a light one.">knockout</code> : <span class="dim">—</span>}</td>
                          <td>{r.state ? <code class="vocab st">{r.state}</code> : <span class="dim">—</span>}</td>
                          <td class="c-value">
                            {node?.isColor && <span class="swatch" style={`background:${node.resolved}`} />}
                            <code>{node?.lineage[0]?.ref ?? '—'}</code>
                            <span class="dim"> · {node?.resolved}</span>
                          </td>
                          <td class="c-used">
                            {node && node.usedByTokens.length === 0 && node.usedByComponents.length === 0 && node.usedByFiles.length === 0 ? (
                              <span class="orphan-flag">orphan</span>
                            ) : (
                              <details class="uses">
                                <summary>
                                  {node && node.usedByTokens.length > 0 && <span class="pill">{node.usedByTokens.length} tok</span>}
                                  {node && node.usedByComponents.length > 0 && <span class="pill">{node.usedByComponents.length} comp</span>}
                                  {node && node.usedByFiles.length > 0 && <span class="pill">{node.usedByFiles.length} file</span>}
                                </summary>
                                {node && node.usedByTokens.length > 0 && <p><b>Tokens:</b> {node.usedByTokens.join(', ')}</p>}
                                {node && node.usedByComponents.length > 0 && <p><b>Components:</b> {node.usedByComponents.join(', ')}</p>}
                                {node && node.usedByFiles.length > 0 && <p><b>Files:</b> {node.usedByFiles.join(', ')}</p>}
                              </details>
                            )}
                          </td>
                        </tr>
                      );
                    })}
                  </tbody>
                </table>
                </div>
              </details>
            ))}
          </div>
        </details>
      ))}
          </div>
          )}

          {cat.kind === 'typography' && (
          <div class="cat-body">
            <h3 class="sub">The slots · intention &gt; variant &gt; property</h3>
            <p class="cat-note">
              The same decomposition the colour tree above uses, on the other half of tier 2. Colour is
              {' '}<code>property &gt; intention &gt; variant &gt; state</code> and roots its tree on the
              property, so its three groups are background/content/border. Typography is
              {' '}<code>intention &gt; variant &gt; property</code> and roots on the
              {' '}<b>intention</b>. Same rule — a tree roots on slot 1 — applied to a different slot 1,
              which is what makes these two categories readable as one system rather than two habits.
            </p>
            <p class="cat-note">
              <b>{t2.typeSlotCoverage.total}</b> composite tokens · <b>{t2.typeSlotCoverage.intentions}</b> intentions ·
              {' '}<b>{t2.typeSlotCoverage.variants}</b> variants.
              {t2.typeSlotCoverage.unparsed.length === 0 ? (
                <> Every one parses into all three slots.</>
              ) : (
                <span class="req"> {t2.typeSlotCoverage.unparsed.length} do not parse: {t2.typeSlotCoverage.unparsed.join(', ')}</span>
              )}
              {' '}The matrix further down answers a different question — <em>is a composite complete?</em> —
              and its unit is the CLASS. This one&rsquo;s unit is the TOKEN, so it is the view that can
              show a name whose slots do not fill.
            </p>
            <p class="cat-gap">
              <b>Composite adoption: {t2.compositeAdoption.usedByComponent} of {t2.compositeAdoption.total}</b> composites are
              applied by a component in the kit ({t2.compositeAdoption.byComponent.map((c) => <code class="vocab">.{c}</code>)}).
              {' '}{t2.compositeAdoption.orphan} are applied nowhere at all.
              {' '}<b>Read the `comp` pill, not the `file` one.</b> A `file` hit is usually this
              documentation rendering a specimen of the composite, which is not evidence that anything
              depends on it — that is exactly why the two are counted separately rather than summed.
              The per-property `Used by` column to the right cannot show any of this: it reports
              {' '}<code>typography.css</code> on all {t2.typeSlotCoverage.total} rows, because a component
              applies the CLASS and never reads the tokens.
            </p>

            {t2.typographyGroups.map((g) => (
              <details class={g.isUnclassified && g.total === 0 ? 'tok-group is-empty' : 'tok-group'} data-group>
                <summary>
                  <code class="group-label">{g.label}</code>
                  <span class="summary-right">
                    <span class={g.isUnclassified && g.total === 0 ? 'group-count none' : 'group-count'}>
                      {g.total}
                    </span>
                  </span>
                </summary>
                {g.isUnclassified && (
                  <p class="cat-note">
                    Nothing should be here, and the emptiness is the claim — every composite name
                    fills slot 1 with a real intention. Anything whose first word is not one lands
                    here instead of being absorbed into a neighbour, exactly as
                    {' '}<code>no intention in slot 2</code> works for colour.
                  </p>
                )}
                <div class="table-scroll">
                  <table class="tok-table">
                    <thead>
                      <tr><th>Variant</th><th>Property</th><th>Token</th><th>References</th><th>Resolves to</th><th>Used by</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                      {g.variants.map((v) => v.rows.map((r, i) => {
                        const node = t2.semanticNodes.get(r.name);
                        return (
                        <tr data-token={r.name}>
                          {i === 0 && (
                            <td rowspan={v.rows.length} class="c-variant">
                              {v.label ? <code class="vocab">{v.label}</code> : <span class="dim">—</span>}
                              <span class="fb">
                                {v.classShipped
                                  ? <>.{v.className}</>
                                  : <span class="req">no class ships this</span>}
                              </span>
                              {/* THE COMPOSITE's own edges. The per-property rows to
                                  the right report `typography.css` and nothing else —
                                  assembling them into a class is that file's whole job
                                  — so the consumer question can only be answered here,
                                  against the CLASS a component actually applies. */}
                              <span class="composite-edges">
                                {/* THE question this cell exists to answer: which
                                    components apply this composite. Spelled out
                                    rather than collapsed behind a count — a list of
                                    component names is the useful artefact, and for
                                    26 of 28 composites that list is EMPTY, which is
                                    a finding you should not have to click to see. */}
                                <span class="used-by-comp">
                                  <span class="wired-label">used by components</span>
                                  {v.usedBy.components.length > 0 ? (
                                    v.usedBy.components.map((c) => <code class="comp-chip">{c}</code>)
                                  ) : (
                                    <span class="pill none">none</span>
                                  )}
                                </span>
                                {v.usedBy.files.length > 0 && (
                                  <details class="uses">
                                    <summary><span class="pill">{v.usedBy.files.length} file</span></summary>
                                    <p><b>Files:</b> {v.usedBy.files.join(', ')}</p>
                                  </details>
                                )}
                                {v.assemblesFrom.length > 0 && (
                                  <span class="wired-ing">
                                    <span class="wired-label">assembles from</span>
                                    {v.assemblesFrom.map((w) => (
                                      <code title={w.name}>
                                        {w.property}: <b>{w.variant}</b>
                                      </code>
                                    ))}
                                  </span>
                                )}
                              </span>
                            </td>
                          )}
                          <td><code class="vocab">{r.property}</code></td>
                          <td class="c-name"><code>{r.name}</code></td>
                          {/* The FIRST hop. For a composite this is normally an
                              ingredient — the edge a spoke re-points to swap a face
                              — so it is shown rather than collapsed into the tier-1
                              terminal beside it. `via tier 1` marks a composite that
                              reaches past the ingredients straight into a primitive,
                              which is legitimate for size/leading/tracking (there is
                              no ingredient layer for those) and worth seeing. */}
                          <td class="c-refs">
                            {r.references ? (
                              <>
                                <code class="vocab">{r.references}</code>
                                {r.references === r.tier1 && <span class="fb">via tier 1</span>}
                              </>
                            ) : <span class="dim">literal</span>}
                          </td>
                          <td class="c-value">
                            {r.tier1 && r.tier1 !== r.references && <code>{r.tier1}</code>}
                            <span class="dim">{r.tier1 && r.tier1 !== r.references ? ' · ' : ''}{r.resolved}</span>
                          </td>
                          <td class="c-used">
                            {node && node.usedByTokens.length === 0 && node.usedByComponents.length === 0 && node.usedByFiles.length === 0 ? (
                              <span class="orphan-flag">orphan</span>
                            ) : (
                              <details class="uses">
                                <summary>
                                  {node && node.usedByTokens.length > 0 && <span class="pill">{node.usedByTokens.length} tok</span>}
                                  {node && node.usedByComponents.length > 0 && <span class="pill">{node.usedByComponents.length} comp</span>}
                                  {node && node.usedByFiles.length > 0 && <span class="pill">{node.usedByFiles.length} file</span>}
                                </summary>
                                {node && node.usedByTokens.length > 0 && <p><b>Tokens:</b> {node.usedByTokens.join(', ')}</p>}
                                {node && node.usedByComponents.length > 0 && <p><b>Components:</b> {node.usedByComponents.join(', ')}</p>}
                                {node && node.usedByFiles.length > 0 && <p><b>Files:</b> {node.usedByFiles.join(', ')}</p>}
                              </details>
                            )}
                          </td>
                        </tr>
                        );
                      }))}
                    </tbody>
                  </table>
                </div>
              </details>
            ))}

            <h3 class="sub">Ingredients · property &gt; variant</h3>
            <p class="cat-note">
              The faces and weights the composites are assembled from. Their shape is
              {' '}<code>property &gt; variant</code> — <b>colour&rsquo;s</b> shape, not the composites&rsquo; —
              because an ingredient has no intention slot: <code>sans</code> is not a job text does.
              That is why they are a separate tree rather than a ninth intention, and why the
              namespace carries two slot orders on purpose.
            </p>
            {t2.typographyIngredients.map((g) => (
              <details class="tok-group" data-group>
                <summary>
                  <code class="group-label">{g.label}</code>
                  <span class="summary-right"><span class="group-count">{g.rows.length}</span></span>
                </summary>
                <div class="table-scroll">
                  <table class="tok-table">
                    <thead><tr><th>Variant</th><th>Token</th><th>References</th><th>Resolves to</th><th>Used by</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                      {g.rows.map((r) => {
                        const node = t2.semanticNodes.get(r.name);
                        return (
                        <tr data-token={r.name}>
                          <td><code class="vocab">{r.variant}</code></td>
                          <td class="c-name"><code>{r.name}</code></td>
                          <td class="c-refs">
                            {r.references ? <code class="vocab">{r.references}</code> : <span class="dim">literal</span>}
                          </td>
                          <td class="c-value">
                            {r.tier1 && r.tier1 !== r.references && <code>{r.tier1}</code>}
                            <span class="dim">{r.tier1 && r.tier1 !== r.references ? ' · ' : ''}{r.resolved}</span>
                          </td>
                          {/* The count that matters most on this table. These are the
                              most directly-read tokens in the system, and that IS the
                              adoption debt the composites exist to retire — a high
                              `comp` number here is the size of that job. */}
                          <td class="c-used">
                            {node && node.usedByTokens.length === 0 && node.usedByComponents.length === 0 && node.usedByFiles.length === 0 ? (
                              <span class="orphan-flag">orphan</span>
                            ) : (
                              <details class="uses">
                                <summary>
                                  {node && node.usedByTokens.length > 0 && <span class="pill">{node.usedByTokens.length} tok</span>}
                                  {node && node.usedByComponents.length > 0 && <span class="pill">{node.usedByComponents.length} comp</span>}
                                  {node && node.usedByFiles.length > 0 && <span class="pill">{node.usedByFiles.length} file</span>}
                                </summary>
                                {node && node.usedByTokens.length > 0 && <p><b>Tokens:</b> {node.usedByTokens.join(', ')}</p>}
                                {node && node.usedByComponents.length > 0 && <p><b>Components:</b> {node.usedByComponents.join(', ')}</p>}
                                {node && node.usedByFiles.length > 0 && <p><b>Files:</b> {node.usedByFiles.join(', ')}</p>}
                              </details>
                            )}
                          </td>
                        </tr>
                        );
                      })}
                    </tbody>
                  </table>
                </div>
              </details>
            ))}

            <h3 class="sub">Composites, grouped by intention</h3>
      <p class="cat-note">
        This section used to open by saying there were <b>zero</b> tier-2 typography tokens — that the
        layer existed only as classes in <code>typography.css</code>, so Figma couldn&rsquo;t consume
        it and a spoke couldn&rsquo;t re-point it. That gap is closed. Every cell below now names a
        real tier-2 token, and the class is nothing but the assembly of them.
      </p>
      <p class="cat-note">
        The matrix is the composite and its parts in one view: the row is the role, each column is one
        property, and each cell shows the <code>--typography-*</code> token that supplies it, the
        hops down to the <b>tier-1 token</b> it lands on (bold), and the value that token resolves
        to — the same reading as the colour rows above, which name their tier-1 referent rather
        than only its value. {t2.typeCoverage.placed} composites across {t2.typeIntentions.length} intentions, grouped by
        the intention DERIVED from each name rather than by an authored list. The six property columns are fixed, so an unset
        property would read as a hole rather than an absence — there are none left: every composite now
        pins all five (six for <code>eyebrow</code>), so no composite inherits a type property from
        whatever it happens to sit inside.
      </p>
      {t2.deprecatedAliases.length > 0 && (
        <p class="cat-gap">
          <b>Not counted above:</b> {t2.deprecatedAliases.length} deprecated aliases, the
          pre-composite class names. They are kept because spoke markup lives outside this repo and
          deleting a selector would silently unstyle live pages rather than fail loudly. All but one
          resolve through the identical tokens as their replacement, so they cannot drift.
          They are parsed separately rather than filtered out, because
          {' '}{t2.deprecatedAliases.length + t2.typeCoverage.placed} classes of which only
          {' '}{t2.typeCoverage.placed} place into an intention reads as unaccounted-for composites
          rather than as aliases doing their job.
          <span class="alias-list">
            {t2.deprecatedAliases.map((c) => <code class="vocab">.{c}</code>)}
          </span>
          <b>To retire them:</b> migrate spoke markup to the composite names (a find-and-replace —
          the hub itself has zero usages), publish the plugin so spoke sessions stop teaching the old
          names, then delete the DEPRECATED block in <code>typography.css</code> and the
          {' '}<code>./type-roles.css</code> entry from the package exports.
        </p>
      )}

      {t2.typeIntentions.map((intent) => (
        <details class="tok-category prop type" data-group open>
          <summary>
            <span class="cat-label">{intent.label}</span>
            <span class="summary-right">
              {intent.inconsistency && <span class="inf-flag">inconsistent</span>}
              <span class="group-count">{intent.roles.length}</span>
            </span>
          </summary>
          <p class="cat-note">{intent.definition}</p>
          {intent.note && <p class="cat-note">{intent.note}</p>}
          {intent.inconsistency && <p class="cat-gap">{intent.inconsistency}</p>}
          <div class="cat-body">
            <table class="tok-table type-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Composite</th>
                  {t2.TYPE_PROPS.map((p) => <th>{p}</th>)}
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {intent.roles.map((role) => (
                  <tr data-token={`.${role.className}`}>
                    <td class="c-name"><code>.{role.className}</code></td>
                    {t2.TYPE_PROPS.map((p) => {
                      const d = role.props[p];
                      if (!d) {
                        const required = (t2.REQUIRED_TYPE_PROPS as readonly string[]).includes(p);
                        return (
                          <td class={required ? 'unset is-required' : 'unset'}>
                            <span class={required ? 'req' : 'dim'}>{required ? 'required' : 'unset'}</span>
                          </td>
                        );
                      }
                      if (d.literal) return <td><code class="vocab warnv">{d.resolved}</code></td>;
                      return (
                        <td>
                          <code class="vocab">{d.ref}</code>
                          {d.fallbackRef && (
                            <span class="fb">↳ fallback <code>{d.fallbackRef}</code></span>
                          )}
                          {d.chain.length > 0 ? (
                            <span class="t1">
                              {d.chain.map((l) => (
                                <>
                                  <span class="arrow">→</span>
                                  <code class={l.tier === 'primitive' ? 't1-ref' : undefined}>{l.ref}</code>
                                </>
                              ))}
                            </span>
                          ) : d.refTier === 'primitive' ? (
                            <span class="t1 direct" title="Reads tier 1 directly — no tier-2 hook for a spoke to re-point">
                              <span class="arrow">→</span>tier 1 direct
                            </span>
                          ) : null}
                          <span class="dim resolved">{d.resolved ?? '?'}</span>
                        </td>
                      );
                    })}
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </details>
      ))}

      {t2.typeCoverage.incomplete.length > 0 && (
        <p class="cat-gap">
          <b>Incomplete composites:</b>{' '}
          {t2.typeCoverage.incomplete.map((r, i) => (
            <>
              {i > 0 && ', '}
              <code>.{r.className}</code> (no {r.missing.join(', ')})
            </>
          ))}
          . A composite is meant to fully determine how text renders. Where a required property is
          unset the role inherits it instead, so the same class renders differently depending on what
          it sits inside — the exact failure a composite exists to prevent.
        </p>
      )}
            <h3 class="sub">Faces &amp; weights &mdash; the ingredients, by property</h3>
            <PropertyLevel props={cat.properties ?? []} />
          </div>
          )}

          {cat.kind === 'family' && (
            <div class="cat-body">
              <PropertyLevel props={cat.properties ?? []} />
            </div>
          )}
        </details>
      ))}
    </Section>

    <Section id="core" title="Tier 1 · Core / universal">
      <p>{coreCategory.note}</p>
      <div class="core-wrap">
        {coreCategory.groups.map((group) => (
          <details class="tok-group is-core" data-group>
            <summary>
              <code class="group-label">{group.label}</code>
              <span class="summary-right">
                <span class="core-flag">core</span>
                <span class="group-count">{group.tokens.length}</span>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="cat-note">{coreSetNotes[group.label]}</p>
            <TokenGroupTable group={group} bare />
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p class="cat-note">
        Verified against <code>themes.css</code>: neither shipped theme (beacon, qanat) re-points any
        token in these sets, so the core contract holds today. Z-index is a further candidate —
        it is theme-invariant here and is conventionally managed as core/shared — but it is left out
        above rather than promoted silently. A third set, the utility palette, used to sit here: it
        matched the <code>--color-status-*</code> aliases, which have since been deleted in favour of
        the tier-2 roles they fed. The concept moved tiers rather than disappearing, and Core is a
        view over tier 1 — so it no longer has members to show.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Section id="primitive" title="Tier 1 · Primitive">
      <p>{TIER_NOTE.primitive}</p>
      <p class="legend">
        <b>Reading these tables.</b>
        <span><code>*</code> after a token name — it carries a <code>$description</code> in the
        DTCG source; hover the row to read it.</span>
        <span><span class="gap-flag">diverges</span> — the STRUCTURE departs from the model
        (a wrong or missing axis), not that work is outstanding. Adoption debt gets its own
        line and no chip, because the two call for opposite responses: rethink the tier, versus
        go and edit call sites.</span>
        <span><span class="orphan-flag">orphan</span> — nothing reads this token.</span>
      </p>
      {primitiveCategories.map((cat) => (
        <details class="tok-category" data-group>
          <summary>
            <span class="cat-label">{cat.label}</span>
            <span class="summary-right">
              {cat.gap && <span class="gap-flag">diverges</span>}
              <span class="group-count">{cat.count}</span>
            </span>
          </summary>
          <p class="cat-note">{cat.note}</p>
          {cat.gap && (
            <p class="cat-gap">
              <b>What we actually ship:</b> {cat.gap}
            </p>
          )}
          {cat.adoption && (
            <p class="cat-adoption">
              <b>Adoption:</b> {cat.adoption}
            </p>
          )}
          {cat.groups.length === 1 && cat.groups[0].label === cat.label.toLowerCase() ? (
            <TokenGroupTable group={cat.groups[0]} bare />
          ) : (
            <div class="cat-body">
              {cat.groups.map((group) => <TokenGroupTable group={group} />)}
            </div>
          )}
        </details>
      ))}
    </Section>
  </article>
  )}
</DocsLayout>

